{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested at exact clean head 851bd1ad2b5427df3d7d9b03a4257298572e97ba, artifact da146c738ed0ce93c2656dc9f1442b71f6256f22. One finding, and it is about what the repair does not cover rather than what it does.\n\nMUST FIX — the repair guards removal but not creation, so it is bypassable in two acts by any ratifier.\n\nThe change makes changing or restoring operator authority require operator standing: decideSupersede now refuses with \"operator standing is required to change an operator grant\" unless the actor holds `operator`. Creating operator authority was not changed. A roster statement's satisfier is still `role:ratifier` (kinds.go:169), and addRoleGrant fires on ratification of any roster statement (fold.go:369-374), so the only gates on minting an operator are that ratifier role and the new beneficiary rule.\n\nThe beneficiary rule blocks `beneficiary == target.record.Actor || beneficiary == record.record.Actor`. It stops self-dealing and nothing else. So an actor holding `ratifier` and not `operator` can:\n\n1. author a roster grant naming a different actor B as `operator`, and ratify it themselves — B is neither the author nor the ratifier, so the check passes;\n2. have B, now an operator, retire the incumbent operator's grant.\n\nThat is precisely the outcome the new supersede rule exists to prevent, reached without ever holding operator standing. It does not even need a confederate: the same ratifier can author and ratify a participant grant for a key it controls, then promote it. Operator implies ratifier, but ratifier does not imply operator (fold.go, addActorRole on an operator grant), so a plain ratifier is a real and distinct standing here.\n\nThe asymmetry is internally inconsistent rather than merely incomplete. The change asserts that operator and ratifier are different standings by demanding operator specifically for supersession; leaving creation at ratifier level says they are interchangeable. Both cannot be right. The fix is presumably to require target-class authority to *create* an authority grant as well as to change one — an operator grant needs an operator to ratify it — but which way to resolve it is your call, and I would rather you chose than have me assume.\n\nCONFIRMED, checked against the code rather than the report.\n\nThe founding seed is genuinely immutable: index 0 must self-seed the operator roster (fold.go:442-447), and governanceTarget walks a supersede or ratify chain back to the roster statement it ultimately changes, refusing when that statement is index 0. The chain walk cannot loop, because targets must already exist when an act is appended.\n\nSelf-authored and self-ratified authority grants are both refused, and both have tests.\n\nThe dormant-grant handling is right, and it was the part I most expected to be subtly wrong. membershipCarriesOperator treats a grant as operator-bearing when its membershipBasis is the statement being restored and the grant is itself directly active — so a dormant operator grant behind a retired membership is recognised on restoration, and restoring it demands operator standing. roleGrantDirectActive correctly separates the grant's own liveness from the membership chain's.\n\nI also checked the unchecked type assertion at fold.go:635, `target.body.(*State)`, and it is safe: decideRatify returns \"only statements may be ratified\" earlier for any non-State target, so the assertion cannot panic. Worth saying because a panic there would take the whole projection down.\n\nTests: go vet clean; internal/workroom passes at this head with the seven new cases, which cover each property the report claims. I did not run the full suite — the change touches the fold, the UI api types and documentation, and internal/workroom is where the behaviour lives, but you should run the rest before merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:da146c738ed0ce93c2656dc9f1442b71f6256f22","branch":"request/governance-authority-repair","head":"851bd1ad2b5427df3d7d9b03a4257298572e97ba","tests":"go vet clean; internal/workroom passes at this head including the seven new governance cases; full suite not run","verdict":"changes-requested"}}